        <div id="safety-lab-environment" class="content-card">
          <h2>Laboratory Safety Environment</h2>
          <p>The laboratory we work in has strict and well-developed safety management. Entry is controlled by access authorization, the areas are clearly divided, and operations of different risk levels are carried out in their corresponding areas. Warning signs for biological hazards, chemical hazards, flammability, corrosion, autoclaving, ultraviolet light, eyewash, first aid, fire extinguishers, emergency exits and waste segregation are posted throughout the laboratory, and SOPs, emergency contacts and safety reminders are posted at key locations <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-2">[2]</a>. A Safety Emergency Response Plan for Teaching Laboratories (Trial) is also posted in a prominent position, compiled in accordance with the Work Safety Law, the Fire Protection Law and the Emergency Response Law of the People's Republic of China and the Regulations on the Safety Management of Hazardous Chemicals; it sets out the emergency organizational structure, the escalation and reporting procedure and 24-hour emergency contact numbers. Fire-fighting facilities are provided close at hand: the hydrant cabinets and extinguisher boxes around the laboratory are grouped together, their doors carry signage and inspection records, and the access to them is kept clear (Figure 1). After entering the laboratory we can quickly identify the sources of risk, the boundaries of operation and the location of both emergency equipment and fire-fighting equipment (Figure 4).</p>
          <p>The laboratory is equipped with clean benches, fume hoods, an autoclave, an eyewash station, an emergency shower, first-aid kits, fire extinguishers, spill kits, absorbent materials, sharps containers, biohazard waste bags and chemical waste containers. Flammable liquids and other hazardous chemicals are kept in a dedicated storage cabinet with the corresponding SDS documentation on hand, and hazardous waste is placed in segregated collection containers and never poured away at will (Figure 3). The eyewash station and emergency shower (Figure 2) are always available. For microbiological work we operate inside a clean bench; for volatile or irritant chemicals we work in a fume hood (Figure 5). Our supervising teachers give safety reminders tailored to each specific experiment, and team members also remind and check one another. After experiments we handle biological waste and chemical waste by category and autoclave materials that need inactivation. Overall, the laboratory's level of safety, its warning system and its emergency provision give us a reliable basis for carrying out this project. On fire safety, the hydrant cabinets and extinguisher boxes are close at hand and the access to them is kept clear; flammable solvents are drawn only as needed and returned to the cabinet after use, and heating equipment is attended while in use and switched off before we leave.</p>
          
