        <div id="safety-personal" class="content-card">
          <h2>Personal Safety</h2>
          <p>All our members have received laboratory safety training and met the laboratory access requirements <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-1">[1]</a><a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-2">[2]</a>. We are familiar with the laboratory safety rules, emergency procedures and personal protective requirements. Before any laboratory activity in this project began, our supervising teachers gave us an additional targeted safety briefing, focusing on the biological materials involved, the risks of the reagents, the boundaries of operation and the record-keeping requirements. During the experiments our activities were recorded and reported, and we had studied the reagents we used in advance to confirm their risks.</p>
          <p>For personal protection we select and correctly wear lab coats, gloves, goggles and masks according to the risk of the specific operation, keep proper working practice inside a clean bench or fume hood, wash our hands properly before and after experiments, and take off our lab coats before leaving the laboratory. We all know where the eyewash station, first-aid kit (Figure 6), fire extinguisher, spill kit and emergency shower are, and in the event of a spill, contact or suspected exposure we know how to respond immediately and report it. During experiments we supervise one another's protective equipment and working state, keep records complete, and never carry out high-risk operations alone. Overall, personal safety is not a paper rule for us but a practical habit and a team consensus in daily experiments.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-project" class="content-card">
          <h2>Project Safety</h2>
          <p>The target detected in this project is the hemagglutinin (HA) protein on the surface of influenza A virus. The HA used in our experiments is purchased; it is not infectious and does not possess the biological activity of influenza A virus. An influenza vaccine based on recombinant HA protein contains only hemagglutinin as a single antigenic protein and no viral genome, so it cannot self-replicate and is itself non-infectious <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-3">[3]</a>. The HA used in this experiment therefore does not produce the infectious characteristics of influenza A virus, cannot infect people and cannot make people ill. We do not handle live virus, we introduce no replication-competent viral sequences, and we introduce no virulence-related genes.</p>
          <p>For the recognition element we use an anti-HA nanobody (VHH) as the recognition domain <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-4">[4]</a>, and the human hM1Dq receptor as the signalling core <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-5">[5]</a>. The PAGER (Programmable Antigen-gated G-protein-coupled Engineered Receptors) receptor uses an "antigen-gated de-repression" logic <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>: in the absence of HA, the MT1 auto-inhibitory peptide occupies the orthosteric site of hM1Dq and holds the receptor at a low background; once HA binds the nanobody, steric hindrance relieves the MT1 inhibition, and the DCZ small molecule then activates hM1Dq <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-7">[7]</a>, triggering the downstream yeast MAPK pathway and <i>FUS1</i> reporter gene output. This self-inhibitory design reduces background leakage and false-positive risk <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>.</p>
          <p>For the chassis we use the non-pathogenic, laboratory-standard <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> BY4741. <i>S. cerevisiae</i> is classed at the lowest risk level in national biosafety listings, and this project is carried out entirely under biosafety level 1 (BSL-1) conditions <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-1">[1]</a>. BY4741 is an internationally used laboratory chassis with a well-defined genetic background; it is naturally auxotrophic for histidine (His), leucine (Leu), methionine (Met) and uracil (Ura), cannot synthesise these four essential nutrients and must obtain them from outside <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-8">[8]</a>. The vector system, the reporter system and the plasmids used for <i>GPA1</i> humanisation/knockout constructed in this study carry LEU, HIS and URA selection markers respectively, complementing the corresponding auxotrophies.</p>
          <p>To ensure biosafety we also designed a marker-removal strategy: the URA marker is removed by 5-fluoroorotic acid (5-FOA) counter-selection <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-9">[9]</a>, returning the engineered strain to a uracil-auxotrophic state after modification, which together with the chassis's original histidine, leucine and methionine auxotrophies makes it a quadruple auxotroph. This multiple-auxotrophy mechanism makes it difficult for the strain to proliferate effectively in environments lacking the corresponding nutrient supplements; conditions that supply all four free nutrients simultaneously and at sufficient concentration do not normally exist in the natural environment, so our engineered yeast survives and proliferates mainly in specially formulated media.</p>
          <figure class="safety-figure">
            <img src="{{ basePath | default('') }}static/image/safety/safety-ex-1.avif" alt="Growth of wild-type Saccharomyces cerevisiae BY4741 on a complete YPD plate and four single-dropout SD plates lacking histidine, leucine, uracil or methionine" width="1100" height="1366" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
            <figcaption>Figure 7. Growth of wild-type <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em> BY4741 on different selective media. The strain forms colonies on complete YPD but not on the four single-dropout SD plates lacking histidine, leucine, uracil or methionine.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>To verify this native auxotrophy experimentally, wild-type <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em> BY4741 was streaked on a complete YPD plate and four different single-dropout SD plates (lacking histidine, leucine, uracil, or methionine, respectively). Growth was observed after incubation. The absence of colonies on the dropout plates confirms the strain's natural auxotrophy, which is the genetic basis on which the marker-removal and quadruple-auxotrophy containment strategy described above is built.</p>
          <figure class="safety-figure">
            <img src="{{ basePath | default('') }}static/image/safety/safety-ex-2.avif" alt="Colony growth on SD-His selection plates after yeast transformation: three experimental plates (A–C) and a negative-control plate with no DNA added (D)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
            <figcaption>Figure 8. Colony growth on SD-His selection plates after yeast transformation. (A, B, C) Experimental transformation plates; (D) Negative-control plate (no DNA added).</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>We further confirmed the stringency of the histidine selection used in our chassis work. Following the transformation of the reporter plasmid into <em>S. cerevisiae</em> BY4741 competent cells, the transformation mixture was plated on SD-His selective medium to screen for positive transformants. As shown in Figure 8, robust colony growth was observed on the three experimental plates (Figure 8A–C), indicating a successful transformation process. In contrast, the negative-control plate (Figure 8D), which lacked the plasmid DNA, showed no colony growth. This absence of colonies on the negative control confirms that the SD-His selection pressure is stringent and there was no contamination or untransformed background growth.</p>
          <p>This design — genetically modifying an already auxotrophic strain and layering a marker-removal step on top — makes it difficult for the strain to proliferate effectively outside the specific supplemented medium, thereby reducing the risk of environmental release. It should be noted that auxotrophy is usually regarded as the first layer of biological containment, and its protective strength depends on the availability of the essential nutrients in the surrounding environment <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-10">[10]</a>; work elsewhere has pushed yeast escape frequencies below 10⁻⁹ by introducing stronger barriers such as dependence on an unnatural amino acid. We therefore do not rest the whole safety case on auxotrophy itself, but also adopt enclosed operation, autoclave inactivation of waste and standardized management, forming multiple layers of protection.</p>
          <p>For chassis modification we use the CRISPR-Cas9 system <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-11">[11]</a> to knock out <i>FAR1</i> <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-12">[12]</a> and <i>SST2</i> <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-13">[13]</a>, removing interference from the yeast's own mating pathway with the cell cycle and signal strength; we also designed a Gpa1-Gαq chimeric G protein, replacing the five C-terminal amino acids KIGII of yeast Gpa1 with EYNLV from human Gαq <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-14">[14]</a>, so that hM1Dq can couple to the yeast MAPK pathway. The reporter module uses the pheromone-responsive <i>FUS1</i> promoter <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-15">[15]</a> to drive <i>yEGFP</i> <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-16">[16]</a> or <i>MEL1</i> <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-17">[17]</a> expression, producing green fluorescence or a blue colour respectively. These modifications target only signal transduction and reporter output; they introduce no pathogenic factors and do not enhance the yeast's pathogenicity or environmental fitness.</p>
          <p>All cultures, consumables and waste containing the engineered yeast are collected according to the laboratory's biological waste rules and autoclaved or otherwise effectively inactivated before disposal. Waste liquids containing chemical reagents are collected separately and handed to a qualified institution. The whole system is modular: replacing only the nanobody domain would allow it to be extended to the detection of other respiratory pathogens <a class="cite-ref" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>, and the safety assessment will be updated in step with any change of target.</p>
        </div>

